<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  The Slipcase media type, for shared-mime-info.

  SPEC 4 names `application/x.slipcase+zip` and the extension `.slpc`, and
  reserves no magic bytes: a container is identified by opening it and finding a
  conformant metadata member, which is not a question this database can ask. The
  glob is therefore the only identification available.

  This file is the one declaration of the type on a Linux machine. Every
  Slipcase product depends on the package carrying it rather than shipping a
  copy, because two packages cannot ship the same path — dpkg refuses the second
  install outright — and the icon a media type is drawn with is one file at one
  path.
-->
<mime-info xmlns="http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/shared-mime-info">
  <mime-type type="application/x.slipcase+zip">
    <comment>Slipcase container</comment>
    <!--
    True and useful. A container is a ZIP, so an archive tool can open one and a
    desktop that knows nothing of slipcases still has something to offer. It
    does not cost the glob: where a name matches `*.slpc` and the content sniffs
    as `application/zip`, shared-mime-info takes the glob, because the
    magic-matched type is the parent rather than the child.

    It carries no icon with it, which is measured and worth stating, because it
    is the reading everybody has first: `content_type_is_a` answers true and the
    icon name list is unaffected. Without the two elements below a container
    draws as a blank generic document rather than as an archive.
    -->
    <sub-class-of type="application/zip"/>
    <glob pattern="*.slpc"/>
    <icon name="application-x.slipcase+zip"/>
    <!--
    Naming this icon again, rather than leaving the default, and the reason is a
    property of how GTK searches. GIO hands the file manager the whole name list
    at once and GTK4 searches theme-major: every name is tried in Adwaita before
    any name is tried in hicolor. The default generic icon for `application/*`
    is `application-x-generic`, which Adwaita has and hicolor's Slipcase icon
    cannot outrank, so the generic won every lookup and containers drew as blank
    pages. Naming the specific icon here leaves Adwaita nothing to answer, so
    hicolor is reached and the drawing appears. Measured both ways, in
    `slipcase-desktop` first and again here.
    -->
    <generic-icon name="application-x.slipcase+zip"/>
  </mime-type>

  <!--
    The payload families, below. Each is a type of its own so that it can carry
    an icon of its own, because an icon belongs to a media type and there is no
    way to draw one file of a type differently from another.

    **Each is a guess, and the guess is from the container's name.** Appendix B
    of SPEC is where `foo.pdf.slpc` holds `foo.pdf` comes from, it is
    non-normative, and SPEC 3 requires a reader to find the payload by
    `payload.file` alone and never by that convention. Nothing here is a reader,
    and an icon is a hint rather than a verdict, but the hint can be wrong:
    a container named `invoice.pdf.slpc` around a PNG payload draws as a PDF.
    The products opening it say what is actually inside, which is the answer a
    person acts on. A thumbnailer would read the metadata and know, and would
    also be an executable run against untrusted archives on whatever is in the
    directory, which SPEC 6 names as a hazard in those words.

    **The longer glob wins**, which is what makes this work at all:
    `report.pdf.slpc` matches both `*.slpc` and `*.pdf.slpc` and
    shared-mime-info takes the one with more literal characters. Measured, along
    with the rest of this: `report.PDF.slpc` matches too, since a glob is
    case-insensitive unless it says otherwise, and `deck.xyz.slpc` falls through
    to the plain type with no declaration needed.

    **`sub-class-of` carries the association**, so a family type is opened by
    whatever opens a container: `gio mime application/x.slipcase-pdf+zip`
    answers with the same default application as the parent. What it does not
    carry is the *recommended* list, which is exact-type only. That is why
    `slipcase-desktop.desktop` and `slipcase-open.desktop` name these types in
    their `MimeType=` lines, and why adding a family here means adding it there.

    **`generic-icon` names the plain container icon** rather than the family's
    own, so a family declared before its drawing exists degrades to the case
    instead of to a blank page. It also keeps `application-x-generic` out of the
    name list entirely, which is what the note above is about.

    **These stay in the unregistered `x.` tree, permanently.** The type being
    registered with IANA is the format; these name a drawing, and minting
    siblings of a registered type to choose an icon would be a misuse of the
    name space. When `application/vnd.excelano.slipcase+zip` is answered, one
    type here moves and these five do not.
  -->

  <mime-type type="application/x.slipcase-pdf+zip">
    <comment>Slipcase container (PDF)</comment>
    <sub-class-of type="application/x.slipcase+zip"/>
    <glob pattern="*.pdf.slpc"/>
    <icon name="application-x.slipcase-pdf+zip"/>
    <generic-icon name="application-x.slipcase+zip"/>
  </mime-type>

  <mime-type type="application/x.slipcase-document+zip">
    <comment>Slipcase container (document)</comment>
    <sub-class-of type="application/x.slipcase+zip"/>
    <glob pattern="*.doc.slpc"/>
    <glob pattern="*.docx.slpc"/>
    <glob pattern="*.odt.slpc"/>
    <glob pattern="*.rtf.slpc"/>
    <glob pattern="*.txt.slpc"/>
    <glob pattern="*.md.slpc"/>
    <glob pattern="*.csv.slpc"/>
    <glob pattern="*.toml.slpc"/>
    <glob pattern="*.json.slpc"/>
    <glob pattern="*.xml.slpc"/>
    <glob pattern="*.yaml.slpc"/>
    <glob pattern="*.yml.slpc"/>
    <icon name="application-x.slipcase-document+zip"/>
    <generic-icon name="application-x.slipcase+zip"/>
  </mime-type>

  <mime-type type="application/x.slipcase-image+zip">
    <comment>Slipcase container (image)</comment>
    <sub-class-of type="application/x.slipcase+zip"/>
    <glob pattern="*.png.slpc"/>
    <glob pattern="*.jpg.slpc"/>
    <glob pattern="*.jpeg.slpc"/>
    <glob pattern="*.gif.slpc"/>
    <glob pattern="*.svg.slpc"/>
    <glob pattern="*.webp.slpc"/>
    <glob pattern="*.tif.slpc"/>
    <glob pattern="*.tiff.slpc"/>
    <glob pattern="*.bmp.slpc"/>
    <glob pattern="*.heic.slpc"/>
    <glob pattern="*.avif.slpc"/>
    <icon name="application-x.slipcase-image+zip"/>
    <generic-icon name="application-x.slipcase+zip"/>
  </mime-type>

  <mime-type type="application/x.slipcase-audio+zip">
    <comment>Slipcase container (audio)</comment>
    <sub-class-of type="application/x.slipcase+zip"/>
    <glob pattern="*.mp3.slpc"/>
    <glob pattern="*.flac.slpc"/>
    <glob pattern="*.wav.slpc"/>
    <glob pattern="*.ogg.slpc"/>
    <glob pattern="*.opus.slpc"/>
    <glob pattern="*.m4a.slpc"/>
    <glob pattern="*.aac.slpc"/>
    <icon name="application-x.slipcase-audio+zip"/>
    <generic-icon name="application-x.slipcase+zip"/>
  </mime-type>

  <mime-type type="application/x.slipcase-video+zip">
    <comment>Slipcase container (video)</comment>
    <sub-class-of type="application/x.slipcase+zip"/>
    <glob pattern="*.mp4.slpc"/>
    <glob pattern="*.mkv.slpc"/>
    <glob pattern="*.webm.slpc"/>
    <glob pattern="*.mov.slpc"/>
    <glob pattern="*.avi.slpc"/>
    <glob pattern="*.m4v.slpc"/>
    <icon name="application-x.slipcase-video+zip"/>
    <generic-icon name="application-x.slipcase+zip"/>
  </mime-type>
</mime-info>
